225:
"A person who has faith, is endowed with virtue, possesses fame and wealth, wherever they frequent, they are honored right there." According to this word of the Buddha, faith is called "wealth (vitta)" because it brings the praise of the world. Furthermore, this wealth of faith is called "supreme (seṭṭha)" because it follows one to the next life, is not shared with others, is the cause of all success, and is the source of even mundane wealth like gold and silver. Indeed, only one with faith performs meritorious deeds like giving (dāna) and thereby acquires wealth; whereas the wealth of one without faith leads only to harm. Therefore, it is called "supreme." The mention of "a man's" is a teaching given by way of a supreme designation; therefore, it should be understood that the wealth of faith is supreme not only for men but also for women and others.

"Dhamma" refers to the ten wholesome courses of action (kusalakammapatha) or the practices of giving, virtue, and mental development (dāna, sīla, bhāvanā). "Well-practiced (suciṇṇo)" means well-performed and well-conducted. "Brings happiness" means it brings human happiness like that of the merchant's son Soṇa or Raṭṭhapāla, divine happiness like that of Sakka and others, and ultimately the happiness of Nibbāna like that of Prince Mahāpaduma and others.

"Truth (sacca)": This word "sacca" is seen in many senses. For instance: in "Speak the truth, do not get angry," it refers to truthful speech (vācāsacca). In "Ascetics and Brahmins stand in truth," it refers to the truth of abstinence (viratisacca). In "Why do the skilled speakers of views speak various truths?" it refers to the truth of views (diṭṭhisacca). In "Monks, these are the four Brahmin truths," it refers to Brahmin truths (brāhmaṇasacca). In "Truth is one, there is no second," it refers to the ultimate truth (paramatthasacca). In "Among the four truths, how many are wholesome?" it refers to the Noble Truths (ariyasacca). In this context, however, truthful speech (vācāsacca) is intended, including within it the ultimate truth of Nibbāna or the truth of abstinence. By the power of that truth, elements like water follow one's will, and beings cross to the other shore of birth, aging, and death. With this meaning in mind, it was said:

"By truthful speech, even water flows;
By truth, the wise overcome even poison;
Because of truth, the rain-god thunders and rains;
Those established in truth aspire for Nibbāna."
